# Cloudflare Pages redirect map — the WordPress URLs this site retires.
#
# This is the Pages equivalent of the .htaccess block in docs/CUTOVER.md.
# Pages never reads .htaccess, so if you change one, change the other, or
# staging stops predicting production.
#
# Two differences from the .htaccess version, both deliberate:
#
# 1. Every source appears twice, with and without the trailing slash.
#    WordPress published trailing-slash URLs, but old links, quotes and pasted
#    addresses turn up both ways and a Pages rule matches the path literally.
#
# 2. The 410 Gone list is not here. _redirects only emits 3xx statuses, so the
#    retired transactional and orphan URLs — /cart-2/, /checkout/, /jobs/,
#    /platform-learn-more/, /sample-page-2/, /elementor-4445/, /top-bar-header/,
#    /home-alternative/, /journey/, /sustainability/, /resources/, /technology/,
#    /staff-leave-request-form/, /meter-inquiry-form-step-3/ — simply match
#    nothing and get the branded 404.html with a 404 status. Google drops a 404
#    as readily as a 410, only slower, and nobody arrives at those URLs with
#    intent. If you want the faster signal, a Cloudflare Snippet on those paths
#    can return 410; it is not worth a Worker on its own.
#
# WARNING — do not add a rule whose source and destination are the same path.
#
# Cloudflare Pages matches these sources ignoring the trailing slash, so a rule
# like `/about/  /about  301` also matches `/about` and redirects it to itself.
# That took /about and /blog down with ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS on 25 August 2026,
# live, for about an hour. Pages already normalises trailing slashes on its own,
# so those rules were never needed in the first place.
#
# The generator that builds this file skips the bare form when it equals the
# destination; it must also skip the trailing-slash form. Both were wrong.

# Two rules used to point at blog articles that do not exist yet -
# /video-burst-geyser-explained/ and /gap-warranty-after-sales-support/ aimed at
# the burst-geyser and warranty posts, which are still unwritten stubs excluded
# from the build. A 301 into a 404 is worse than no redirect: the reader hits
# nothing and the link equity is lost. Both now land on /blog. Repoint them at
# the articles when the articles exist.

# The old B2B URLs point at the home page, NOT at /insurers.
#
# /insurers sits behind Cloudflare Access with a one-time PIN, so sending an
# old insurer URL there gave anyone arriving from Google a 403 wall instead of
# a page - which is how a broker following a years-old link met a locked door
# on 25 August 2026. The gate stays (the page carries the fee bands, the float
# mechanics and HA-COM-2026-001), so the redirects go somewhere a stranger can
# actually read.
#
# The footer link "Insurers and brokers" still points at /insurers on purpose:
# someone who chooses it is a lead, and the PIN entry lands in the Access log.
#
# If /insurers is ever split into a public overview plus a gated
# /insurers/commercial, point these six back at /insurers.

# /leak-detection has no rule any more - it is a real page again as of
# 25 August 2026, reusing the URL the WordPress site had. A redirect here
# would shadow the page: Pages resolves this file before it serves assets,
# so the rule wins and the page becomes unreachable.

# The apex domain is NOT handled here — a _redirects file cannot match on
# hostname. homeassist.co.za -> www is a Cloudflare Redirect Rule. See the
# runbook.

